-## Subagent dispatch requires multi-agent support
+# *Codex tools for Theseus*

-Add to your Codex config (`~/.codex/config.toml`):
+*Read notes with normal file tools and use exact vault-relative paths from the existing ticket JSON. Run the installed router by passing a JSON object on stdin to `python3 <absolute-installed-using-theseus>/scripts/routing.py`. Use a quoted argument for an absolute path containing spaces. The router only returns a decision. It never reads the vault, edits files, performs Git operations or executes the verification command.*

-```toml
-[features]
-multi_agent = true
-```
+| *Input field* | *Value and evidence* |
+| --- | --- |
+| *`ticket_path`* | *Exact path from the activation.* |
+| *`write_targets`* | *Array of paths the selected request authorizes changing. Exclude links or files consulted for context.* |
+| *`block_candidates`, `selected_block`* | *Verified `Sources/` block-note paths tied to the ticket note, the plan's Building block link or authorized writes. For multiple candidates, use the exact note independently selected by the user or unambiguously identified by the ticket.* |
+| *`work`, `action`* | *Work is `first`, `modification`, `refactoring` or `bug_fix`. Action is `auto`, `discuss`, `plan`, `execute` or `continue`. Use `auto` when the development stage follows from work and context. Send `refactoring` only for an explicit request.* |
+| *`plan`* | *Optional object with `path`, `block`, `work`, `status`. Derive it from the verified plan, Specification, Acceptance and recorded execution status. Use `ready` only after validating a complete plan that can start. Check execution progress independently of the user-managed frontmatter Status. Other states are `draft`, `in_progress`, `interrupted`, `blocked`, `done`, `rejected`. For execute or continue from outside `Plans/`, include the exact plan path in `write_targets` after verifying that the ticket authorizes updating it.* |
+| *`requirements_ready`, `diagnosis_confirmed`* | *Set true only with evidence of sufficient agreed requirements for the requested work or a confirmed cause, respectively.* |
+| *`execute_after_plan`* | *Set true only when the user explicitly says to continue from planning through implementation without stopping. Validate the completed plan and its scope first.* |
+| *`local`* | *Optional object with the exact scoped implementation or test `paths` also present in `write_targets`, a concrete reproducible `verification` command, and boolean `independent` and `single_block` evidence. Use only when required behavior and checks are already clear and the Specification needs no change.* |
+| *`refactor_confirmed`, `refactor_workspace_ready`* | *Set true only after explicit user confirmation of the requested refactor and cleanup, and verification of the isolated prepared worktree, respectively.* |

-This enables the multi-agent tools that skills like
-`dispatching-parallel-agents` and `subagent-driven-development` use.
-Which tools you get depends on the multi-agent version your model
-preset selects (current presets run V2; older ones run V1). Trust your
-actual tool list over any table — including this one — when they
-disagree.
-
-- **Spawning:** give children a clean context with
-  `spawn_agent {fork_turns: "none"}`; the default `"all"` copies your
-  entire transcript into the child. On Codex 0.145+, role files under
-  `~/.codex/agents/` attach to isolated forks via `agent_type`.
-  Full-history forks accept `model` and `reasoning_effort` overrides
-  (only `agent_type` is refused there) — isolated forks are the SDD
-  default for context hygiene, not because overrides require them.
-- **Fix rounds:** resume the implementer with `followup_task` — it
-  delivers your message, triggers a turn, and transparently reloads a
-  child the harness evicted. Never dispatch a fresh implementer on the
-  theory that a spawned agent cannot be messaged again; on V2 it
-  always can.
-- **Lifecycle:** V2 has no `close_agent`. Finished children are
-  evicted automatically when slots are needed; leaving them unclosed
-  costs nothing. Only V1 sessions have `close_agent` — there, close
-  reviewers when their review returns, and close each implementer
-  after its task's review passes.
-- **Model names:** never copy a model name from a skill, table, or old
-  session into `spawn_agent` without checking it against your current
-  spawn allowlist — V2 accepts only V2-capable presets and hard-errors
-  on the rest.
